From 8ec0ed34edeeeff1e60b0405776e4f48a119ed48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 11:10:49 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] bun:ffi cc(): compile with the system C compiler where
 TinyCC has no backend

cc() was the last thing in bun:ffi still going through TinyCC, and TinyCC has
no code generator for ppc64le, android or freebsd -- `cfg.tinycc` is false on
all three and cc() threw "not available in this build".

It does not need one. bun 1.4.3 already has two FFI paths and only cc() used
the old one: dlopen() resolves each symbol with dlsym and hands the pointer to
Bun__CreateJSCFFIFunction, where JavaScriptCore builds the JS<->C thunk from a
JSC::FFI::Signature. So where ENABLE_TINYCC is false, cc() now compiles the
user's sources with the system C compiler into a temporary shared object,
dlopens it, and takes the same route. Nothing of TinyCC's scaffolding is
reproduced: no generated JSFunctionCall wrapper, no FFI.h, no libtcc1.c
compiler-rt, no ffi-std*.h stand-in headers. Where TinyCC exists, nothing
changes.

$CC first, then clang, cc, gcc. $CC set and not found is an error rather than
a reason to silently use a different compiler. Flags: -shared -fPIC, then
either the caller's `flags`, or $BUN_CC_OPTIONS, or -std=c11 -O2 -g -- the
same precedence the TinyCC path gives `flags` and $BUN_TCC_OPTIONS.
$BUN_TCC_OPTIONS itself is deliberately not read here; its documented value
carries TinyCC-only spellings like -Wl,--export-all-symbols that a real linker
rejects. Compiler diagnostics go to bun's stderr, which is better than
re-wrapping them: the user gets clang's output verbatim. The object is
unlinked once dlopen has it.

No compiler version is required. Measured on POWER9/ppc64le with clang 22.1.8
and gcc 16.1.1, both producing the same answer; every flag used predates clang
3.1 and gcc 4.7. Packaging: an unversioned optdepends on clang, no upper
bound.

One thing the shared-object path cannot carry: a napi_env argument. TinyCC's
wrapper materialised it from an injected symbol, and a JSC FFI signature has
no slot for one (FFI::Type reserves the tag). napi_value is a JSValue and
works. cc() now says so instead of failing obscurely, and the tests that need
napi_env skip on `hasTinyCC`, along with the ones asserting TinyCC's own
behaviour -- that _Thread_local is rejected, that a compiler-runtime header
directory is staged. A real compiler does neither.

src/js/bun/ffi.ts stops assigning `native` onto a symbol that already has one:
JSC's FFI functions carry `native` and `ptr` as readonly intrinsics, and
overwriting one throws in a builtin's strict mode.

Measured on ppc64le, against WebKit ppc64le-port 8aee24c4ec:
  test/regression/issue/26249.test.ts   2 pass 0 fail  (was 2 fail)
  test/js/bun/ffi/cc.test.ts           21 pass 0 fail  (was the whole file)
  test/napi/napi-value-ffi.test.ts      1 pass 0 fail
